61 592748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167902 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dc1ec685-f38d-51a2-9240-4f3a1f1346e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167902 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167903 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167903 #edb/ref 1061 592751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167903 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3e7ba88a-596c-53db-b70b-6b89ee975318" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167903 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168038 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167958 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167958 #edb/ref 1061 608067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167958 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "81f0071e-ac02-5b8f-ba91-88209136e903" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167958 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167959 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167959 #edb/ref 1061 608068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167959 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d95ffe14-fb14-518a-ac26-6970a9d57acf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167959 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168037 #edb/ref 1064 "In a homogeneous boundary value problem (BVP) that depends on a parameter $\\lambda$, most values of $\\lambda$ lead only to the trivial solution $y\\equiv 0$.  \n\nThe special values of $\\lambda$ that produce *nontrivial* solutions are called **eigenvalues**, and the corresponding nontrivial solutions are called **eigenfunctions**.\n\nFor example, consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\ny'' - 18\\lambda y' + 81\\lambda^2y  = 0, \\quad y(0) = 0, \\quad 4y(3) + 5y'(3) = 0, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\quad \\lambda \\in\\mathbb R.\n$$\n\nLet's find the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. \n\nThe method for solving the differential equation depends on whether $\\lambda = 0$ or $\\lambda \\neq 0.$ To find the eigenvalues, we must therefore investigate these two possibilities as separate cases.\n\nOn the next slide, we will analyze the case where $\\lambda = 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168037 #edb/ref 1065 14646 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168037 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1378993d-8bac-50d2-b1ea-4f7c3a1f5ec2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168037 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168038 #edb/ref 1064 "When the auxiliary equation has purely imaginary roots, the solutions are trigonometric, and the boundary conditions usually force $\\lambda$ to take on a discrete set of values.\nSo, we again look for values of $\\lambda$ that allow a nontrivial solution.\n\nWe will now find the **eigenvalues** and **eigenfunctions** for the boundary value problem:\n\n$$\ny'' + 9\\lambda^2 y = 0, \\qquad y'(0) = 0, \\quad y(4) = 0, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq 4, \\quad \\lambda \\geq 0.\n$$\n\nWe consider two cases based on the value of $\\lambda,$ since they correspond to different forms of the general solution.\n\n**Case 1: $\\lambda = 0$**\n\nSetting $\\lambda = 0$ gives the equation $y'' = 0.$\nIntegrating this equation twice gives the general solution:\n\n$$\ny = Ax + B.\n$$\n\nTo apply the boundary conditions, we first find the derivative, $y' = A.$\n\n- Applying $y'(0) = 0$ gives $A = 0.$ The solution becomes $y = B.$\n\n- Applying $y(4) = 0$ then gives $B = 0.$\n\nThis yields the trivial solution $y \\equiv 0.$ Since an eigenfunction must be nontrivial, $\\lambda = 0$ is *not* an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2: $\\lambda > 0$**\n\nThe auxiliary equation is $m^2 + 9\\lambda^2 = 0,$ which has roots $m = \\pm 3i\\lambda.$\nThe general solution is therefore\n\n$$\ny = A\\cos (3\\lambda x) + B\\sin (3\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nNow we apply the boundary conditions. First, we find the derivative:\n\n$$\ny' = -3A\\lambda\\sin(3\\lambda x) + 3B\\lambda\\cos(3\\lambda x).\n$$\n\n- Applying the condition $y'(0) = 0$ yields:\n\n$$\n-3A\\lambda\\sin(0) + 3B\\lambda\\cos(0) = 0 \\quad\\implies\\quad 3B\\lambda = 0.\n$$\n\nSince we are in the case where $\\lambda > 0,$ this requires $B = 0.$ The solution simplifies to:\n\n$$\ny = A\\cos (3\\lambda x).\n$$\n\n- Next, applying the condition $y(4) = 0$ yields:\n \n\n$$\nA\\cos (12\\lambda) = 0.\n$$\n\n For a nontrivial solution, we must have $A \\neq 0,$ which means we require\n \n\n$$\n\\cos (12\\lambda) = 0.\n$$\n\n This condition is met when $12\\lambda$ is an odd multiple of $\\pi/2:$\n\n$$\n12\\lambda = \\dfrac{(2n+1)\\pi}{2}, \\quad \\text{for } n = 0, 1, 2, \\dots\n$$\n\nSolving for $\\lambda$ gives the set of **eigenvalues**:\n\n$$\n\\lambda_n = \\dfrac{(2n+1)\\pi}{24}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding **eigenfunctions** are \n\n$$\ny_n(x) = A_n \\cos(3\\lambda_n x) , \\quad A_n \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168038 #edb/ref 1065 14650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168038 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2e52b01a-4f53-5d27-9984-dd731308d87d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168038 #edb/ref 1067 "Trigonometric Eigenfunctions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168055 #edb/ref 1064 "Next, we consider the case $\\lambda \\neq 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions of \n\n$$\ny'' - 18\\lambda y' + 81\\lambda^2y  = 0,\n$$\n\nfor $\\lambda \\neq 0,$ we set $y = e^{m x}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-18\\lambda m+81{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-9\\lambda )(m-9\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives a repeated root $m = 9\\lambda.$ The general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{9\\lambda x} + Bxe^{9\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nNow we apply the boundary conditions.\n\n- Applying the first boundary condition, $y(0) = 0,$ yields\n\n$$\ny(0) = Ae^{0} + B(0)e^{0} = 0 \\quad \\implies \\quad A = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, the solution simplifies to $y = Bxe^{9\\lambda x}.$ Its derivative is\n\n$$\ny' = Be^{9\\lambda x} + 9B\\lambda xe^{9\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\n- Applying the second boundary condition, $4y(3) + 5y'(3) = 0,$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(3B{e}^{27\\lambda })+5(B{e}^{27\\lambda }+27B\\lambda {e}^{27\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ 12B{e}^{27\\lambda }+5B{e}^{27\\lambda }+135B\\lambda {e}^{27\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ B{e}^{27\\lambda }(17+135\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a nontrivial solution (an eigenfunction), we require $B\\neq 0.$ Since $e^{27\\lambda}$ is never zero, we must have\n\n$$\n17+135\\lambda = 0 \\quad \\implies \\quad \\lambda = -\\dfrac{17}{135}.\n$$\n\nThis is our **eigenvalue**. The corresponding **eigenfunction** is\n\n$$\ny(x) = Bxe^{9(-17/135) x} = Bxe^{-17x/15}.\n$$\n\nNote that any nonzero constant multiple of this function is also an eigenfunction." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168055 #edb/ref 1065 14892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168055 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f4d9fb35-7991-5b49-bfd6-c82ad2ef1c69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168055 #edb/ref 1067 "Non-Trivial Solution of the Eigenvalue Problem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168056 #edb/ref 1064 "We begin by considering  the case $\\lambda = 0.$\n\nSetting $\\lambda = 0$ in the original equation, $y'' - 18\\lambda y' + 81\\lambda^2y  = 0,$ gives a much simpler equation:\n\n$$\ny'' = 0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation twice with respect to $x$ gives the general solution\n\n$$\ny = Ax + B.\n$$\n\nNow we apply the boundary conditions. \n\n- The first condition, $y(0) = 0,$ implies:\n\n$$\ny(0) = A(0) + B = 0 \\quad \\implies \\quad B=0.\n$$\n\nSo the solution must be of the form $y = Ax.$\n\n- For the second condition, we first find the derivative, $y' = A.$ Applying the condition $4y(3) + 5y'(3) = 0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(A\\cdot 3)+5(A) & =0 \\\\ 12A+5A & =0 \\\\ 17A & =0\\,\\,⟹\\,\\,A=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both $A=0$ and $B=0,$ we are left with the trivial solution $y \\equiv 0.$ By definition, an eigenfunction must be a *nontrivial* solution.\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda = 0$ is *not* an eigenvalue. \n\nNext, we will check the case where $\\lambda \\neq 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168056 #edb/ref 1065 14893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168056 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b3458843-3f77-548b-b991-69c3d163d75d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168056 #edb/ref 1067 "Trivial Solution of the Eigenvalue Problem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314744 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314744 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314744 #edb/ref 1069 "q-338904" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314744 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314744 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-12\\lambda {y}^{′}+36{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314744 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b2709e91-93e6-520b-8d50-587e1e5c8629" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314744 #edb/ref 1074 "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ and $y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-12\\lambda m+36{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-6\\lambda )(m-6\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=6\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{6\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{6\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{6\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{6\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =6A\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda x}+B{e}^{6\\lambda x}+6B\\lambda x{e}^{6\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6A\\lambda +B & =0 \\\\ (6\\lambda )A+B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{6\\lambda }+B{e}^{6\\lambda }+2(6A\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda }+B{e}^{6\\lambda }+6B\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+B+2(6A\\lambda +B+6B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+B+12A\\lambda +2B+12B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+12\\lambda )A+(3+12\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(6\\lambda )A+B=0 \\\\ (1+12\\lambda )A+(3+12\\lambda )B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}6\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1+12\\lambda  & 3+12\\lambda \\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\lambda (3+12\\lambda )-(1)(1+12\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ 18\\lambda +72{\\lambda }^{2}-1-12\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ 72{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda -1 & =0 \\\\ (12\\lambda -1)(6\\lambda +1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{6}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{12}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{6}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(6\\lambda )A+B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A+B=0\\,⇒\\,A=B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=B{e}^{-x}(x+1).\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{12},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(6\\lambda )A+B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}A+B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-2B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-2B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-2) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $B{e}^{-x}(x+1)\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/2}(x-2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365858 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186365859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365858 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186365864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365858 #edb/ref 1069 "q-338845" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365858 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365858 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-10\\lambda {y}^{′}+25{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365858 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c6e87982-ab6f-5e96-94e7-996ae7f3b605" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365858 #edb/ref 1074 "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ and $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-10\\lambda m+25{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-5\\lambda )(m-5\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=5\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{5\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{5\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{5\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =5A\\lambda {e}^{5\\lambda x}+B{e}^{5\\lambda x}+5B\\lambda x{e}^{5\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5A\\lambda {e}^{5\\lambda }+B{e}^{5\\lambda }+5B\\lambda {e}^{5\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 5A\\lambda +B+5B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (5\\lambda )A+(1+5\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{10\\lambda }+2B{e}^{10\\lambda }+2(5A\\lambda {e}^{10\\lambda }+B{e}^{10\\lambda }+10B\\lambda {e}^{10\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+2(5A\\lambda +B+10B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+10A\\lambda +2B+20B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+10\\lambda )A+(4+20\\lambda )B & =0 \\\\ (1+10\\lambda )A+4(1+5\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(5\\lambda )A+(1+5\\lambda )B=0 \\\\ (1+10\\lambda )A+4(1+5\\lambda )B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}5\\lambda  & 1+5\\lambda  \\\\ 1+10\\lambda  & 4(1+5\\lambda )\\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\lambda \\cdot 4(1+5\\lambda )-(1+5\\lambda )(1+10\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (1+5\\lambda )[20\\lambda -(1+10\\lambda )] & =0 \\\\ (1+5\\lambda )[20\\lambda -1-10\\lambda ] & =0 \\\\ (1+5\\lambda )(10\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{5}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{10}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{5}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{5{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{5{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(5\\lambda )A+(1+5\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A+(1-1)B=0\n$$\n\n \nwhich gives $A=0,$ and where $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=Bx{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{10},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{5{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{5{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(5\\lambda )A+(1+5\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}A+\\frac{3}{2}B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-3B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-3B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-3) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $Bx{e}^{-x}\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/2}(x-3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365859 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365859 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365859 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365859 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365859 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365859 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365859 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186365861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365859 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "76d0668d-3ca7-569