=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}(\\pi )=0$ yields $A=0.$ Thus, we have a nontrivial solution, so $\\lambda =0$ is a parameter value corresponding to the eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+16{\\lambda }^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 4\\text{i}\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\lambda x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\lambda x)\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\lambda x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-4A\\lambda \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\lambda x)+4B\\lambda \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-4A\\lambda \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+4B\\lambda \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $B=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(\\pi )=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-4A\\lambda \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\lambda \\pi )=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n4\\lambda \\pi =n\\pi \n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{n}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{\\lambda }_{n}x),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391696 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391696 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391696 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391699 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391696 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391696 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391701 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391696 #edb