"56ed424b-8ca9-59d2-8528-1ebfe5b468f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391720 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186391721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391720 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186391726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391720 #edb/ref 1069 "q-338920" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391720 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391720 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16\\lambda y=0,\\,y(0)=y(\\pi )=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi ,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in N.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391720 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "55c03286-efa5-5f72-9411-1febab9b4b0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391720 #edb/ref 1074 "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda >0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)=y(\\pi )=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ does not correspond to an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+16\\lambda  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 4\\text{i}\\sqrt{\\lambda }.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda }),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda })\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda })+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(\\pi )=0$ yields\n\n$$\nB\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi \\sqrt{\\lambda })=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n4\\pi \\sqrt{\\lambda }=n\\pi \n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}}{16}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}}),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391721 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391721 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391721 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391721 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391721 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391721 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391721 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186391724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391721 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c3e4d0a8-336d-51eb-9840-10ac5a3bc5ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391722 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{{n}^{2}}{8}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391722 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391722 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d299aab6-7d45-5daf-aeed-dbcaf20eb01f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391723 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391723 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391723 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "50cf2632-5224-53c3-9780-700b593c95e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391724 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{{n}^{2}}{16}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391724 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 1759218604